 @Thorocious  Yep I have a theory in response to your question. Many PC enthusiasts own stocks and/or real estate. Those people aren't all that bothered by the cost of living crisis because over the last few years their investments have gone up by more than living expenses have (though that could change if inflation remains high for a few more years).

Somewhere around 55% of Americans own stocks, and the S&P 500 has gone up by nearly 200% in the last 10 years. In my country (Australia) the figures aren't quite that good (about 50% of Aussies own stocks and our benchmark has gone up about 140% over 10 years) but still impressive nonetheless. There's a lot of people out there who have been quietly racking up big gains over the years, you just don't hear about it because the media would rather focus on the crisis for the sake of sensationalism, as the media are wont to do.

If those people want to upgrade their PC, they might grumble a bit about the price, but pay it anyway, as they feel they can afford it because the prices of their assets have gone up as well. This is an economic principle called the wealth effect, I'm sure AMD and Nvidia know this, and I suspect it's part of the reason they set the prices where they did.

